T19.01|Equity securities convey ownership or equity-related rights. Common shares generally hold residual claims; preference shares have specified priority or distribution features; depositary receipts represent interests through a depositary structure. Voting, conversion, redemption and currency rights vary by instrument.~~Read actual terms rather than assuming every share class has equal economics. A quoted price per security must be adjusted for the number of underlying shares represented.|Common shares: Residual ownership with stated voting rights.;;Preference shares: Priority features under contractual terms.;;Depositary receipts: Tradable representation of specified underlying shares.|One depositary receipt represents two ordinary shares. Each ordinary share is valued at ₹100 before exchange, fees and other differences.~~Underlying share reference is ₹200 per receipt.;;Compare the receipt's price after currency conversion and costs.;;Assess rights, settlement and access differences before calling a gap arbitrage.~~Security-unit conversion is essential for comparison.~~Market frictions can justify temporary differences.|Read rights and conversion ratios.;;Match currencies and units.;;Assess priority, dilution and settlement structure.
T19.02|Primary markets issue new equity, raising money for the issuer or distributing securities under the transaction terms. Secondary markets transfer existing holdings among investors and support price discovery and liquidity. A traded purchase does not usually send its price to the company.~~Offerings can mix newly issued shares and selling-shareholder shares. Distinguish where proceeds go and whether the transaction dilutes existing ownership.|Primary issuance: New securities created.;;Secondary trading: Existing securities change owners.;;Mixed offering: New issuance and existing-owner sales together.|An offering sells 100 shares at ₹10: 60 newly issued and 40 sold by an existing owner. Ignore fees.~~Company gross proceeds are ₹600.;;Selling owner's gross proceeds are ₹400.;;Only the 60 new shares increase total share count.~~The full ₹1,000 offering size is not all company funding.~~Fees and special terms change net proceeds and ownership.|Identify issuer and sellers.;;Reconcile proceeds and share count.;;Separate capital raising from liquidity transfer.
T19.03|IPOs introduce shares to public markets; follow-on offers raise or sell equity after listing; rights issues give eligible holders subscription opportunities; private placements target selected investors under relevant rules. Each affects funding, ownership and pricing differently.~~Existing shareholders are diluted by new shares unless they participate or receive economically offsetting rights. A discount to the old price should be assessed with the value of subscription rights and new cash.|IPO or follow-on: Public offer at different listing stages.;;Rights issue: Proportional subscription opportunity under terms.;;Private placement: Negotiated issuance to selected participants.|A firm has 100 shares worth ₹10 each and issues 25 new shares at ₹8. Assume no other value change or fees.~~New cash is ₹200.;;Post-issue value is ₹1,200 across 125 shares, or ₹9.60 each.;;Analyse rights allocation to determine individual holder effects.~~A discounted new issue changes per-share value under the simple model.~~Actual pricing also reflects information, use of funds and market conditions.|Read proceeds and eligibility terms.;;Model fully diluted ownership.;;Assess funding purpose, fees and rights.
T19.04|Stock exchanges organise trading under their rules; indices represent selected securities under defined weighting and maintenance methods. Market capitalisation is price multiplied by shares outstanding under the stated share definition. It measures equity value, not enterprise value or funds raised.~~A rising share price can increase market cap without cash entering the company. Index membership does not imply a stock is suitable for every investor.|Exchange: Trading and market-rule venue.;;Index: Rules-based market representation.;;Market capitalisation: Aggregate quoted equity value.|A company has 10 lakh shares at ₹100.~~Market capitalisation is ₹10 crore.;;If price rises to ₹120 with unchanged shares, market cap becomes ₹12 crore.;;Company cash is unchanged by this price move alone.~~Market value can change without a financing transaction.~~Multiple share classes and treasury holdings require consistent counting.|Verify share definitions and price date.;;Distinguish equity from enterprise value.;;Read index selection and weighting rules.
T19.05|Free float measures shares available for trading under an index or analytical definition, excluding specified restricted or strategic holdings. Free-float market-cap weighting adjusts constituent value for this eligible share base. Equal weighting and price weighting produce different exposures.~~Float definitions differ among providers. Market-cap weight changes with prices and corporate actions, and periodic rebalancing can create trading needs.|Full-cap weighting: All eligible outstanding shares.;;Free-float weighting: Adjust for specified availability.;;Equal or price weighting: Alternative rules.|A company has 10 lakh shares priced ₹100 and a 40% index-eligible float.~~Full market cap is ₹10 crore.;;Float-adjusted cap is ₹4 crore.;;Use ₹4 crore in that index's stated float-weight calculation.~~The adjustment changes index influence without changing the firm's total equity value.~~Actual provider rules determine strategic and restricted exclusions.|Read the provider methodology.;;Verify float and share counts.;;Assess concentration and rebalancing effects.
T19.06|Dividends pay resources to owners; splits change share units; bonus issues increase shares through a specified equity reclassification; buybacks reduce outstanding shares under their terms. Mechanical corporate actions do not automatically create total economic value.~~Prices may adjust around distributions and unit changes, while information or financing effects can create additional market moves. Total-return analysis includes distributions rather than looking only at price.|Cash distribution: Funds leave the company.;;Split or bonus: Ownership units change.;;Repurchase: Company acquires shares and uses resources.|An investor owns 10 shares at ₹100 before a 2-for-1 split. Assume no other value change.~~Share count becomes 20.;;Reference price becomes ₹50.;;Holding value remains ₹1,000.~~More shares do not by themselves mean greater wealth.~~Taxes, market reactions and nonmechanical events require separate analysis.|Adjust units and prices consistently.;;Include distributions in return.;;Separate mechanics from information effects.
T19.07|A market order prioritises execution at available prices; a limit order restricts acceptable price but may not execute. Stop conditions trigger specified order behaviour, which can differ across venues. Bid-ask spread, depth and gaps affect actual fills.~~A stop price is not necessarily a guaranteed execution price. Liquidity can vanish or prices can gap, and partial fills can leave residual exposure.|Market order: Accept available execution prices.;;Limit order: Restrict price under the stated direction.;;Stop-triggered order: Activation follows specified conditions.|Best ask is ₹100 for 10 shares, then ₹102 for 20. A market buy seeks 15.~~First 10 cost ₹1,000.;;Next 5 cost ₹510.;;Average execution price is ₹1,510/15 ≈ ₹100.67.~~A displayed best price need not fill the entire order.~~Fees and venue execution rules add further costs or constraints.|Identify execution versus price priority.;;Inspect depth and spread.;;Read trigger, expiry and partial-fill rules.
T19.08|Margin trading borrows or uses collateral to increase exposure; short selling sells borrowed or otherwise deliverable securities with an obligation to return them. Leverage amplifies gains and losses, while margin requirements can force action before the intended horizon.~~A short position can face rising-price losses, borrowing fees, recall and dividend-related obligations. Rules and eligibility differ by market; an illustration is not an instruction to trade.|Long leverage: Larger asset exposure than owned capital.;;Short exposure: Benefit from price decline with return obligation.;;Margin constraint: Collateral required to maintain the position.|Buy ₹100 of stock using ₹50 equity and ₹50 debt, ignoring interest. Price falls 20%.~~Asset value falls to ₹80.;;Debt remains ₹50.;;Equity becomes ₹30, a 40% loss on the initial ₹50.~~Leverage doubles this percentage loss in the simplified case.~~A margin call can occur before an eventual recovery.|Model losses relative to own capital.;;Include financing and collateral terms.;;Assess forced-liquidation and borrowing risks.
T19.09|Securities lending temporarily transfers securities under an agreement requiring return of equivalent assets, usually with collateral and fees. It supports short sales and settlement but creates counterparty, collateral and operational exposure. Ownership rights and distributions are handled by contractual and market rules.~~Collateral reduces but does not eliminate risk. Its value can move with the borrowed asset, and liquidation may involve delays or gaps.|Lender: Supplies securities and receives agreed compensation.;;Borrower: Owes equivalent return and relevant obligations.;;Collateral: Protection subject to eligibility and valuation.|Shares lent are worth ₹100 lakh and collateral ₹105. Prices rise so the shares become worth ₹112 before collateral updates.~~Protection shortfall relative to share value is ₹7.;;Apply margining and recovery procedures under the agreement.;;Assess collateral liquidity and counterparty capacity.~~Initial overcollateralisation can disappear as prices move.~~Legal netting, recall and settlement arrangements determine actual exposure.|Read collateral and return terms.;;Monitor valuations and margin.;;Assess rights, fees and default procedures.
T19.10|Fundamental analysis studies the business, financial statements, competitive position and valuation to estimate future cash and risk. A strong company can be an unattractive investment at an excessive price; a low price can reflect genuinely deteriorating economics.~~Separate the investment thesis from a list of favourable facts. The analysis should explain expectations embedded in price and what evidence would change the view.|Business analysis: Revenue, costs and competitive durability.;;Financial analysis: Cash, capital and obligations.;;Valuation analysis: Price versus expected benefits and risk.|A business earns ₹10 lakh, growing sustainably at 5%, while its price assumes much faster growth under a reverse model.~~Analyse customer and reinvestment evidence for 5% or a higher path.;;Test value under realistic scenarios.;;Identify what growth the quoted price requires.~~Business quality and valuation attractiveness are separate judgments.~~Forecasts remain uncertain; a single ratio cannot settle the thesis.|Understand operating mechanics.;;Reconcile earnings with cash and capital.;;Compare price-implied and evidence-supported expectations.
T19.11|Industry analysis examines rivalry, entry, substitutes, customer power and supplier power alongside demand and regulation. Competitive advantage is a durable ability to earn favourable economics, not merely current market share. Switching costs, scale, brand or access can help but may erode.~~Link qualitative advantages to measurable pricing, retention, costs and returns. A claim of a moat should explain why competitors cannot cheaply replicate or neutralise it.|Cost advantage: Lower resource cost for comparable value.;;Differentiation: Customer willingness to pay or retention.;;Structural protection: Access, network or contractual barriers.|Firm A charges ₹10 and costs ₹6 per unit; rivals charge ₹10 and cost ₹8.~~A contribution is ₹4 versus rivals' ₹2.;;Investigate whether the ₹2 cost advantage is durable and replicable.;;Assess capital needed to maintain it.~~Higher margins can reflect a competitive mechanism.~~Temporary input prices or underinvestment can imitate an advantage.|Identify the mechanism and evidence.;;Measure persistence and reinvestment.;;Test competitive responses and erosion.
T19.12|An investment thesis states why the security's future outcomes differ from current expectations. A catalyst may cause information or value realisation; risk factors describe how the thesis can fail. The case should include evidence, valuation and explicit disconfirmation conditions.~~A catalyst is not a guaranteed price move. The event may already be priced, arrive late or reveal worse information.|Thesis: Evidence-based expectation gap.;;Catalyst: Event affecting recognition or realisation.;;Risk and falsifier: Conditions weakening the case.|A thesis expects margin recovery from 10% to 15% after a plant ramp-up.~~Identify utilisation and cost evidence supporting recovery.;;Model value at 10%, 12% and 15%.;;Set a review condition if utilisation or unit cost fails to improve by the relevant milestone.~~A testable thesis is more useful than simply calling the stock cheap.~~Price response also depends on market expectations and other events.|State the expectation gap.;;Connect catalysts to economics.;;Record evidence that would reverse the view.
T19.13|Earnings estimates forecast results; an earnings surprise compares actual with a stated prior expectation. Market reaction depends on what was priced, guidance and result quality, not only the headline beat. Consensus averages may hide wide disagreement.~~Compare like-for-like adjusted or reported measures and preserve estimate dates. A beat caused by a reserve release differs from one caused by stronger customer demand.|Consensus estimate: Aggregated defined analyst forecasts.;;Reported surprise: Actual minus matching prior estimate.;;Guidance change: Updated management outlook.|Expected EPS is ₹10 and actual ₹11, including ₹2 unusual gain per share.~~Headline surprise is +10%.;;Underlying EPS under the supported exclusion is ₹9.;;Review future guidance and the source of the gain before interpreting the announcement.~~A headline beat can coexist with weaker recurring performance.~~Different adjustment conventions can change the comparison.|Match metrics and periods.;;Use pre-announcement estimates.;;Analyse quality and forward implications.
T19.14|An equity research report presents business economics, forecasts, valuation, catalysts and risks with traceable sources. It should distinguish facts, analyst estimates and assumptions and explain the path from evidence to conclusion.~~A target price without its valuation basis and horizon is difficult to assess. Include sensitivity, ownership and relevant conflicts rather than treating a polished narrative as proof.|Company and industry section: Business context.;;Financial and valuation section: Forecasts and pricing logic.;;Risk and monitoring section: Failure paths and evidence to revisit.|A report estimates equity ₹500 lakh and 50 lakh diluted shares.~~Implied value per share is ₹10.;;Show the enterprise-equity bridge and share-count definition.;;Explain downside assumptions rather than presenting ₹10 as a precise certainty.~~Readers should be able to reproduce the central calculation.~~Real research requires current filings and dates; examples here are hypothetical.|Cite primary data and dates.;;Reconcile forecasts and valuation.;;State limitations, risks and review conditions.
T19.15|Growth, value, quality, momentum and income styles emphasise different characteristics. They are descriptions of selection approaches, not mutually exclusive guarantees of performance. A high-quality firm can be expensive; a value screen can identify a business in decline.~~Style exposures change through market regimes and portfolio construction. Evaluate risks, costs and diversification rather than expecting one label to remain superior.|Growth or quality: Expansion or durable profitable economics.;;Value: Price low relative to selected fundamentals.;;Momentum: Persistence in selected market performance.;;Income: Distribution-oriented exposure.|A stock trades at low P/B but repeatedly earns below cost of equity.~~Identify why the book-value discount exists.;;Test whether returns can improve or capital can be recovered.;;Compare with a higher-multiple firm earning sustainable excess returns.~~Low valuation ratios do not automatically mean undervaluation.~~Every style needs definitions and economic context.|Define the style screen.;;Assess hidden risks and overlap.;;Compare after-cost results against an appropriate benchmark.
T19.16|Technical analysis studies market prices, volumes and related patterns to describe or forecast trading behaviour. Trend and momentum measures can be tested systematically, while subjective chart interpretation may be difficult to reproduce. Neither replaces analysis of cash flows or obligations.~~Backtests must avoid hindsight, data snooping and unrealistic execution. Apparent patterns can disappear after costs or outside the original sample.|Trend indicator: Direction over a defined window.;;Momentum measure: Relative or absolute recent performance.;;Pattern interpretation: Shape-based hypothesis requiring validation.|A rule buys when a short moving average exceeds a long one.~~Specify windows and decision timing before testing.;;Trade only using data available at that time.;;Include spread, slippage and a separate evaluation period.~~A chart rule becomes assessable when its execution is explicit.~~A historical profitable result does not guarantee persistence.|Define reproducible rules.;;Use point-in-time data.;;Assess costs, multiple testing and out-of-sample results.
T19.17|Shareholding patterns reveal ownership concentration, institutional holdings and changes over time. Promoter pledging uses shares as collateral and can create margin or control risk if prices fall. Pledged shares remain ownership claims until contractual consequences occur.~~Concentration may support long-term governance or weaken minority protections depending on behaviour and rights. Do not infer wrongdoing solely from a pledge percentage.|Strategic ownership: Control or long-term influence.;;Public or institutional holdings: Other shareholder groups.;;Pledged ownership: Shares supporting a borrowing agreement.|A controlling owner pledges shares worth ₹100 lakh against ₹50 debt. The shares fall to ₹60.~~Collateral coverage falls from 2× to 1.2×.;;Read lender margin requirements and top-up rights.;;Assess possible forced sale or control implications.~~Share-price declines can create funding stress for the owner.~~Actual thresholds, other collateral and disclosures determine the risk.|Track ownership and pledge changes.;;Read collateral terms.;;Assess governance and forced-sale channels separately.
